# Navigation

Navigation has two layers: the app shell (where am I in the product)
and within-page structure (where am I in this record). Each layer has
fixed components — pages do not invent navigation.

## The app shell

- The `sidebar` carries the product's sections: one entry per
  top-level area, each with its lucide icon and label, current section
  marked. It collapses to icons; it never scrolls horizontally and
  never nests more than one level.
- The `header` carries global, section-independent tools: patient
  search, the notification drawer (a sheet), the theme toggle and the
  user menu. Page-specific actions never live in the header.
- Page titles repeat in the content region (`<h1>`), not only in the
  shell — the page must make sense with the sidebar collapsed.

## Within a page

| Need | Use |
| ---- | --- |
| Switch between views of the same record | `tabs` |
| Long content, sections consultable independently | `accordion` |
| A long collection, consumed page by page | `pagination` |
| Jump back along the path | breadcrumb links in the page header |

Tabs versus accordion: tabs for parallel *views* the user switches
between (summary / timeline / documents); accordion for stacked
*sections* read in place. If users need two open at once, it is an
accordion (`multiple: true`), never tabs.

## Rules

- Navigation state is in the URL: the active tab, the current page
  number and active filters survive reload and are shareable. Turbo
  navigation, not client-side state.
- `pagination` renders the window it is handed and nothing else — the
  controller decides the window; the partial does no page maths.
- Never disable a navigation item to mean "not available here" — omit
  it. Disabled affordances belong to forms, not navigation.
- The current location is always visibly marked (active sidebar item,
  active tab, current page button) and exposed via `aria-current` /
  `aria-selected`.
